-- THE CONTROL for #1468, with EXACTLY ONE variable flipped: the sibling constructor
-- `B` is gone, so `T` is single-constructor and every value reaching `getX` really does
-- have an `x`.  Same field name, same offset, same `getX`, same two calls' worth of
-- work — and here the answer is genuinely `a=7 v=99`, because `B { z = 99 }` has become
-- `A { x = 99 }`.
--
-- ⚠️ IT PRINTS THE SAME BYTES AS THE PINNED FILE ON PURPOSE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
-- POINT.  `main.mdk`'s `v=99` is `B`'s slot 0 read as if it were `x`; this file's `v=99`
-- is an `x`.  The control therefore proves the machinery (field selection, the `Int`
-- printer, the build) is intact, so a broken control means the ENVIRONMENT broke rather
-- than that #1468 was fixed.  What a FIX to #1468 changes is `main.mdk`'s verdict — it
-- must stop being an accepted program with a fabricated value — while this file must
-- keep compiling and printing `a=7 v=99`.
data T =
  | A { x : Int }

mkA : T
mkA = A { x = 7 }

getX : T -> Int
getX t = t.x

main : <IO> Unit
main = putStrLn ("a=" ++ intToString (getX mkA) ++ " v=" ++ intToString (getX (A { x = 99 })))
